package pages

import "github.com/whale-net/everything/libs/go/htmxui"

// The product-wide Tasks/Board region's view model. The pure builder that
// populates it lives in //krill/ui (product_task_scope.go); this file owns
// only the shape and the rendering, and the shared scope control lives
// beside it in product_task_scope.templ.
//
// Rows arrive in the store read's own order and are rendered in it: the
// read's sort (milestone position descending, then id, then creation, then
// id) is the one order every consumer of the pagination token agrees on, so
// re-sorting here would put the table's rows in an order no continuation
// token can resume.

// ProductTasksAnchor is the DOM id of the region's content: the id the
// scope control's and Refresh's hx-target points at, and the root id of the
// fragment an htmx request gets back. A fragment without it would delete
// its own swap target on the next request (see SpecInlineError).
const ProductTasksAnchor = "krill-product-tasks"

// ProductTaskScopeLabel is one scope mode's name as the control offers it.
type ProductTaskScopeLabel struct {
	// Mode is the store scope kind's wire value -- the same string the
	// query parameter carries.
	Mode string
	// Label is what the operator reads, e.g. "All incomplete milestones".
	Label string
	// Active marks the mode this request resolved to.
	Active bool
}

// ProductTaskContainerOption is one selectable milestone or milepebble.
type ProductTaskContainerOption struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Selected bool

	// OutOfScopeSuffix marks a container the product-wide all-incomplete
	// scope would NOT have included -- shipped or abandoned. FR 7191dba1
	// still lets the operator pick one explicitly, so it stays in the list
	// rather than disappearing; saying so is what stops it reading as a
	// contradiction. Empty for every container that scope would include.
	OutOfScopeSuffix string
}

// ProductTaskRow is one task in the Tasks table (FR f41a352d).
//
// ClaimID and LeaseExpiresAt are the identity the READ observed, kept on
// the row as data attributes so a later write can be claim-guarded against
// a claim that has since changed. They are set together or not at all --
// store.ProductTaskRow guarantees the two agree.
type ProductTaskRow struct {
	// ID is the task's own id, the row's data-krill-task-id.
	ID string

	// Title links to the task's detail. DetailPath is the PRODUCT-scoped
	// detail URL, not the per-container one: this table is not scoped to a
	// container, so a row in it may belong to any milestone under the
	// product, and a link that named a container would name the wrong one
	// for every row but one.
	Title      string
	DetailPath string

	// Milestone is the row's own milestone name. A milepebble's task
	// carries its PARENT milestone here, because that is the container the
	// operator chose a scope by.
	Milestone string

	// Milepebble is the row's own milepebble's name, empty for a task
	// scoped to a milestone directly (an uncut milestone has none).
	Milepebble string

	// Lane and Badges render through the shared TaskLaneBadge /
	// TaskStateBadge components, so this table cannot drift from the board
	// or the detail on either value's colour.
	Lane   string
	Badges []TaskBadge

	// Attempts is the count against the cap, e.g. "2 of 3".
	Attempts string

	// ClaimID and LeaseExpiresAt are what the read observed. Empty
	// LeaseExpiresAt means the row holds no claim, which is what keeps the
	// cell from claiming a lease it does not have.
	ClaimID        string
	LeaseExpiresAt string
}

// ProductTaskRegion is the Tasks/Board region's view model.
type ProductTaskRegion struct {
	// Product is the banner for the product the URL named.
	Product ProductHeader

	// View is "Tasks" or "Board" -- which of the two views of this one
	// scope is being served, so the region can mark itself.
	View string

	// Path is this region's own URL, so the swap control can re-request it.
	Path string

	// RefreshPath is Path plus this request's own query, so a manual
	// Refresh re-reads the scope and filters the URL currently names rather
	// than silently resetting them to the product-wide default.
	RefreshPath string

	// Scope is the control both views share. It is the same value on the
	// Tasks page and the Board page for the same URL, because both build
	// it from the same resolved scope rather than each describing its own.
	Scope ProductTaskScopeControl

	// ScopeLabel is the scope this request resolved to, read back in prose:
	// "All incomplete milestones", or a container's own name.
	ScopeLabel string

	// Lane is the selected lane filter's name, empty for "Any lane".
	Lane string

	// OnlyStuck mirrors the checked filter.
	OnlyStuck bool

	// Total is the count read for this scope's filters -- the "Y" the page
	// footer will read beside the rows it has not rendered yet.
	Total int

	// Rows are the page's tasks, in the read's own order. A cut milestone's
	// tasks are already aggregated by the read (ProductTaskScopeMilestone
	// means the milestone AND its milepebbles), so every row here names the
	// milepebble it came from rather than the table hiding a milestone's
	// work behind a link.
	Rows []ProductTaskRow

	// Empty is set when the scope resolved and holds no tasks, so the region
	// says so instead of reading as a page that failed.
	Empty bool

	// EmptyDetail is that empty state's second sentence, and it NAMES the
	// active filters rather than saying "nothing matches" (FR 61d7fb7b).
	// "No tasks" alone is ambiguous between a scope with no work and a
	// filter that excluded everything the operator can see; naming the
	// scope and the lane is what tells them which control to change.
	EmptyDetail string

	// UpdatedAt is the read instant as an absolute RFC3339 string. It is
	// what the region renders and what the relative "Updated N ago" text is
	// derived from client-side: the server never emits a now-derived string
	// inside a fragment htmx may re-swap, because that string would go stale
	// the moment it is written and the region gives no way to notice.
	UpdatedAt string

	// Error is a read failure in the operator's words, empty on success.
	// Distinct from Empty: one is an answer, the other is a failure.
	Error string
}

// ProductTasks renders the product-wide Tasks/Board region: the scope it
// resolved to, the shared scope control, the total behind it, and the
// region's own answer -- a table of rows, an empty state, or a failure.
//
// The control is inside the region, not beside it, so the hx-get that
// changes the scope swaps both the answer and the control that produced it
// in one move: after the swap the marked mode and the selected option are
// the ones the new URL resolved to, never the ones the old URL did.
templ ProductTasks(d ProductTaskRegion) {
	<section id={ ProductTasksAnchor } data-krill="product-tasks" data-krill-view={ d.View }>
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-4">
			<div>
				<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold" data-krill="page-title">{ d.View }</h2>
				<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-base-content/70">
					Scoped to
					<span data-krill="scope-label">{ d.ScopeLabel }</span>
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="flex items-center gap-3">
				@ProductTasksFreshness(d.UpdatedAt)
				<button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm shrink-0" data-krill="refresh"
					hx-get={ templ.URL(d.RefreshPath) }
					hx-target={ "#" + ProductTasksAnchor }
					hx-swap="outerHTML">
					Refresh
				</button>
			</div>
		</div>
		@ProductTaskScopeForm(d.Scope)
		<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-base-content/70">
			<span data-krill="task-count">{ itoa(d.Total) }</span>
			tasks in scope
			if d.Lane != "" {
				<span data-krill="lane-filter">in lane { d.Lane }</span>
			}
			if d.OnlyStuck {
				<span data-krill="only-stuck">that are stuck</span>
			}
		</p>
		if d.Error != "" {
			<div class="mt-3" data-krill="product-tasks-error">
				@htmxui.Alert(htmxui.AlertError, d.Error, templ.Attributes{
					"data-krill": "product-tasks-error-alert",
				})
			</div>
		} else if d.Empty {
			<div class="mt-3" data-krill="product-tasks-empty">
				@htmxui.EmptyState(
					"No tasks in this scope",
					d.EmptyDetail,
					"", "", templ.Attributes{},
				)
			</div>
		} else {
			@ProductTaskTable(d.Rows)
		}
	</section>
}

// ProductTaskTable is the Tasks view's rows, in the order the read returned
// them.
//
// The whole table is one horizontal-scroll container rather than a fixed
// grid, so a narrow viewport scrolls the table and not the page -- the same
// rule the Board follows for its five columns.
templ ProductTaskTable(rows []ProductTaskRow) {
	<div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
		<table class="table table-zebra" data-krill="task-table">
			<thead class="sticky top-0 bg-base-100">
				<tr>
					<th>Task</th>
					<th>Milestone</th>
					<th>Milepebble</th>
					<th>Lane</th>
					<th>State</th>
					<th class="text-right">Attempts</th>
					<th>Lease</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				for _, t := range rows {
					<tr class="hover" data-krill="task-row"
						data-krill-task-id={ t.ID }
						data-krill-claim-id={ t.ClaimID }
						data-krill-lease-expires-at={ t.LeaseExpiresAt }>
						<td>
							<a href={ templ.URL(t.DetailPath) } class="link font-medium" data-krill="task-title">{ t.Title }</a>
						</td>
						<td data-krill="task-milestone">{ t.Milestone }</td>
						<td data-krill="task-milepebble">{ t.Milepebble }</td>
						<td>@TaskLaneBadge(t.Lane)</td>
						<td>@TaskBadges(t.Badges)</td>
						<td class="text-right" data-krill="task-attempts">{ t.Attempts }</td>
						<td data-krill="task-lease">{ t.LeaseExpiresAt }</td>
					</tr>
				}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
}

// ProductTasksFreshness is the region's single "Updated N ago" element
// (NFR 7b497d92).
//
// The markup carries the read instant and nothing else: the relative text
// is derived in the browser by the shell's own upgrade script, which lives
// in the document head rather than in this fragment. A server-rendered "8
// seconds ago" would be wrong the moment htmx swapped the region in, and
// nothing inside the region would ever reveal it -- the fragment carries no
// clock of its own. The absolute instant stays in the title attribute, so
// the exact read time survives whether or not the script ran.
templ ProductTasksFreshness(updatedAt string) {
	<span class="text-sm text-base-content/70" data-krill="updated-at"
		data-krill-updated-at={ updatedAt } title={ updatedAt }>
		Updated { updatedAt }
	</span>
}

// ProductTasksInlineError is the fragment an htmx caller gets when the
// scope or the read failed: a 200 carrying the failure inline, because
// htmx does not swap on a non-2xx and the operator would otherwise be left
// clicking Refresh with no feedback at all.
//
// It keeps the region's anchor id for the same reason SpecInlineError does
// -- a fragment without it deletes its own swap target.
//
// Its own marker is product-tasks-scope-error rather than the region's
// product-tasks-error: both are alerts, but one is a scope the URL named
// that this product does not own and the other is a read that failed, and
// FR 61d7fb7b requires the three states never be confused. Sharing the
// marker would make them indistinguishable in the markup even though the
// sentences differ.
templ ProductTasksInlineError(anchor, message string) {
	<section id={ anchor } data-krill="product-tasks">
		<div data-krill="product-tasks-scope-error">
			@htmxui.Alert(htmxui.AlertError, message, templ.Attributes{
				"data-krill": "product-tasks-scope-error-alert",
			})
		</div>
	</section>
}